=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
After exhaustive live diagnostics, no fault is present in this dc_clos_bgp
topology. Connectivity between pc_0_0 (10.0.0.2) and pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) is
fully operational in both directions with zero packet loss and the expected
RTT (~1 ms). A traceroute confirms the data plane follows the correct 4-hop
intra-pod path: pc_0_0 → leaf_router_0_0 → spine_router_0_0 → leaf_router_0_1
→ pc_0_1. The super_spine_router_0 is not in this data path and is not
dropping any traffic.

All seven BGP eBGP sessions across the fabric are established and stable. The
kernel routing tables on every device contain only legitimate BGP-learned
routes; there are no null0 / blackhole routes anywhere. leaf_router_0_0
correctly originates 10.0.0.0/24 and learns 10.0.1.0/24 via both spines;
leaf_router_0_1 mirrors this symmetrically. Both spine routers carry correct
best-path routes directly from the leaf routers (1-hop AS paths), and the
super_spine receives those prefixes from the spines as expected.

The technician's "black hole at the top box" diagnosis is not supported by any
evidence. The super_spine has a BGP config with no `network` or `redistribute`
statements (it relies on eBGP propagation from the spines), but this is
architecturally correct for a transit super-spine in a single-pod Clos; it has
no reason to originate routes and is not in the data path for intra-pod
traffic. There is no corrective action required — the fabric is healthy. The
user's original complaint was likely caused by a transient condition during
BGP initial convergence after the lab was first started (sessions had been up
~5 minutes at the time of investigation), which resolved on its own.
=== END ===
